#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712367 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(2 y - 1)\\,i+(2 x - y + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(- 2 t - 2)\\,i+(- t - 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 2,\\quad y=- t - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(2 y - 1)\\,i+(2 x - y + 1)\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(2 \\left(- t - 1\\right) - 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\left(2 \\left(- 2 t - 2\\right) - \\left(- t - 1\\right) + 1\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 2 t - 3)\\,i+(- 3 t - 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t - 2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t - 1)\\,j \\\\  & =(-2)\\,i+(-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({(- 2 t - 3)\\,i+(- 3 t - 2)\\,j}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({(-2)\\,i+(-1)\\,j}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t - 3)\\cdot(-2)+(- 3 t - 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(-1)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\left({4 t + 6}\\right)+\\left({3 t + 2}\\right) \\\\ &=7 t + 8\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left({7 t + 8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{\\frac{7 t^{2}}{2} + 8 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((8)+\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right))-((0)+(0)) \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{23}{2}\\right)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{23}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712374 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y)\\,i+(- x - 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t + 2)\\,i+(2 t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t + 2,\\quad y=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- y)\\,i+(- x - 2)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\left(t + 2\\right) - 2\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 t - 1)\\,i+(- t - 4)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t + 2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 1)\\,j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({(- 2 t - 1)\\,i+(- t - 4)\\,j}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({(1)\\,i+(2)\\,j}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 2 t - 1)\\cdot(1)+(- t - 4)\\cdot(2) \\\\ &=\\left({- 2 t - 1}\\right)+\\left({- 2 t - 8}\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t - 9\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left({- 4 t - 9}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- 2 t^{2} - 9 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((-9)+(-2))-((0)+(0)) \\\\ &=(-11)-(0) \\\\ &=-11\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712378 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y)\\,i+(- 2 x)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t - 2)\\,i+(2 t + 2)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t - 2,\\quad y=2 t + 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 2 y)\\,i+(- 2 x)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 \\left(t - 2\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(- 4 t - 4)\\,i+(- 2 t + 4)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t - 2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t + 2)\\,j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({(- 4 t - 4)\\,i+(- 2 t + 4)\\,j}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({(1)\\,i+(2)\\,j}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 4 t - 4)\\cdot(1)+(- 2 t + 4)\\cdot(2) \\\\ &=\\left({- 4 t - 4}\\right)+\\left({- 4 t + 8}\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t + 4\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left({- 8 t + 4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- 4 t^{2} + 4 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((-4)+(4))-((0)+(0)) \\\\ &=(0)-(0) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712387 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- y + 1)\\,i+(- x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(- t)\\,i+(- t - 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- t,\\quad y=- t - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- y + 1)\\,i+(- x + 1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(- t - 1\\right) + 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\left(- t\\right) + 1\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =(t + 2)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t - 1)\\,j \\\\  & =(-1)\\,i+(-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({(t + 2)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({(-1)\\,i+(-1)\\,j}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(t + 2)\\cdot(-1)+(t + 1)\\cdot(-1) \\\\ &=\\left({- t - 2}\\right)+\\left({- t - 1}\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t - 3\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left({- 2 t - 3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- t^{2} - 3 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((-1)+(-3))-((0)+(0)) \\\\ &=(-4)-(0) \\\\ &=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712400 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y + 2)\\,i+(- 2 x - 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t - 1)\\,i+(2 t - 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$\n\n*Hint:* Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm d r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt\n$$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t - 1,\\quad y=2 t - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(- 2 y + 2)\\,i+(- 2 x - 2)\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(- 2 \\left(2 t - 1\\right) + 2\\right)\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\left(- 2 \\left(t - 1\\right) - 2\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 4 t + 4)\\,i+(- 2 t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t - 1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t - 1)\\,j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({(- 4 t + 4)\\,i+(- 2 t)\\,j}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({(1)\\,i+(2)\\,j}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 4 t + 4)\\cdot(1)+(- 2 t)\\cdot(2) \\\\ &=\\left({- 4 t + 4}\\right)+\\left({- 4 t}\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t + 4\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left({- 8 t + 4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- 4 t^{2} + 4 t}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=((-4)+(4))-((0)+(0)) \\\\ &=(0)-(0) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712445 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x + 1)\\,i+(- 2 y - 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2 \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(- 2 x + 1)\\,i+(- 2 y - 1)\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(- 2 \\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\left(- 2 \\left(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) - 1\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 1) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - 2 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} +\\left({\\begin{aligned}&2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - 2 t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left({\\begin{aligned}&t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- t^{2} + t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&((- 4 \\pi^{2})+(0)+(- 2 \\pi)) \\\\ &{} -((0)+(0)+(0))\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 4 \\pi^{2} - 2 \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=- 2 \\pi \\left(1 + 2 \\pi\\right)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe offset terms integrate by the reverse product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- t^{2} + t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712453 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + 1)\\,i+(y + 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\pi,2 \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(x + 1)\\,i+(y + 2)\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(\\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\left(\\left(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + 2\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} +\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} + t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&((2 \\pi^{2})+(0)+(4 \\pi)) \\\\ &{} -(\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}\\right)+(0)+(- 2 \\pi))\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(4 \\pi + 2 \\pi^{2})-(- 2 \\pi + \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{2}) \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\pi \\left(\\pi + 4\\right)}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe offset terms integrate by the reverse product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t  \\\\ &{} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712463 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(2 x - 1)\\,i+(2 y - 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\pi,2 \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(2 x - 1)\\,i+(2 y - 2)\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(2 \\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) - 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\left(2 \\left(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) - 2\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 1)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 1) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} + 2 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} +\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} + 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t  \\\\ &{} - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left({\\begin{aligned}&2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t  \\\\ &{} - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{t^{2} - t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&((4 \\pi^{2})+(0)+(- 4 \\pi)) \\\\ &{} -((\\pi^{2})+(0)+(2 \\pi))\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 4 \\pi + 4 \\pi^{2})-(2 \\pi + \\pi^{2}) \\\\ &=3 \\pi \\left(-2 + \\pi\\right)\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe offset terms integrate by the reverse product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(t^{2} - t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t  \\\\ &{} - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712465 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n{\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x + 2)\\,i+(- 2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\pi,2 \\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t)) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=(- 2 x + 2)\\,i+(- 2 y)\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(- 2 \\left(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + 2\\right)\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\left(- 2 \\left(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\right)\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2)\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({\\begin{aligned}&(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i \\\\ &{} +(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} +(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ &{} \\cdot(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - 2 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right) \\\\ &{} +\\left({\\begin{aligned}&2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}  \\\\ &{} - 2 t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C F\\cdot\\text{d}r \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\left({2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- t^{2} + 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &=((- 4 \\pi^{2})+(0))-((- \\pi^{2})+(0)) \\\\ &=(- 4 \\pi^{2})-(- \\pi^{2}) \\\\ &=- 3 \\pi^{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe offset terms integrate by the reverse product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- t^{2} + 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712528 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x+2y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\cos t\\,i+t\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve, $x=t\\cos t$ and $y=t\\sin t$. Substitute into both mixed field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=(t\\cos t+t\\sin t)\\,i+(t\\cos t+2t\\sin t)\\,j\\\\&=t(\\cos t+\\sin t)\\,i+t(\\cos t+2\\sin t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach path component needs the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t\\cos t)&=\\cos t-t\\sin t,\\\\\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t\\sin t)&=\\sin t+t\\cos t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\nr^{\\prime}(t)=(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\,i+(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\,j.\n$$\n\nMultiply matching components. Keep the two products factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)&=t(\\cos t+\\sin t)(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\\\&\\quad+t(\\cos t+2\\sin t)(\\sin t+t\\cos t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r&=\\int_0^1\\bigl[t(\\cos t+\\sin t)(\\cos t-t\\sin t)\\\\&\\qquad+t(\\cos t+2\\sin t)(\\sin t+t\\cos t)\\bigr]\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNo antiderivative or further expansion is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712535 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i-xy\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t^2\\,i+t^3\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it." :question/worked-solution "Use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=t^2$ and $y=t^3$ into both components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))&=(t^2+t^3)\\,i-(t^2)(t^3)\\,j\\\\&=(t^2+t^3)\\,i-t^5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both path components:\n\n$$\nr^{\\prime}(t)=2t\\,i+3t^2\\,j.\n$$\n\nThe dot product includes both coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)&=(t^2+t^3)(2t)+(-t^5)(3t^2)\\\\&=2t^3+2t^4-3t^7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{0}^{1}(2t^3+2t^4-3t^7)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712539 :question/problem "Which ordinary definite integral represents $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=y^2\\,i-x^2\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+t^3\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$?\n\nUse the supplied parameterization and limits. Select the integral whose integrand is $F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)$; do not evaluate it.\n\n*Hint: $r^{\\prime}(t)=i+3t^2\\,j$. Substitute the coordinates into the field, then take the dot product.*" :question/worked-solution "For a vector field, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve, $x=t$ and $y=t^3$, so\n\n$$\nF(r(t))=(t^3)^2\\,i-t^2\\,j=t^6\\,i-t^2\\,j.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the path gives\n\n$$\nr^{\\prime}(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}(t^3)}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j=i+3t^2\\,j.\n$$\n\nMultiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)&=t^6(1)+(-t^2)(3t^2)\\\\&=t^6-3t^4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep the supplied parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_{0}^{1}(t^6-3t^4)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712546 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=\\mathbf F=2xy\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2+2yz)\\,\\mathbf j+(y^2-2z)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSelect the family of functions whose gradient is $\\mathbf F$. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The components of the gradient give\n\n$$\nf_x=2xy,\\qquad f_y=x^2+2yz,\\qquad f_z=y^2-2z.\n$$\n\nIntegrate the first equation with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^2y+g(y,z).\n$$\n\nThe integration term is a function of $y$ and $z$, since every term independent of $x$ vanishes when differentiated with respect to $x$. Differentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+g_y(y,z)&=x^2+2yz,\\\\\ng_y(y,z)&=2yz,\\\\\ng(y,z)&=y^2z+h(z).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f=x^2y+y^2z+h(z)$. The third component determines the remaining function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_z&=y^2+h'(z),\\\\\ny^2+h'(z)&=y^2-2z,\\\\\nh'(z)&=-2z,\\\\\nh(z)&=-z^2+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^2y+y^2z-z^2+C.\n$$\n\nChecking all components gives $f_x=2xy$, $f_y=x^2+2yz$, and $f_z=y^2-2z$. Stopping after the first integration would miss the terms needed by the other two components."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712554 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=\\mathbf F=2xy\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2+z)\\,\\mathbf j+(y+2z)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the first component with respect to $x$ and matching the second component has already given\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^2y+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nUse the third component to determine $h(z)$ and select the resulting family of functions. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The components of $\\mathbf F$ require $f_x=2xy$, $f_y=x^2+z$, and $f_z=y+2z$. The supplied expression already satisfies the first two equations: differentiating $x^2y+yz+h(z)$ gives $f_x=2xy$ and $f_y=x^2+z$.\n\nDifferentiate it with respect to $z$ and compare with the third component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_z&=y+h'(z),\\\\\ny+h'(z)&=y+2z,\\\\\nh'(z)&=2z.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nh(z)=\\int 2z\\,\\mathrm dz=z^2+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitution gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^2y+yz+z^2+C.\n$$\n\nIts three partial derivatives are $2xy$, $x^2+z$, and $y+2z$, as required. An additive constant has zero gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712560 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=\\mathbf F=yz e^{xz}\\,\\mathbf i+(e^{xz}+z)\\,\\mathbf j+(xy e^{xz}+y-2z)\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSelect the family of functions whose gradient is $\\mathbf F$. In each choice, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We need\n\n$$\nf_x=yz e^{xz},\\qquad f_y=e^{xz}+z,\\qquad f_z=xy e^{xz}+y-2z.\n$$\n\nFor the first integration, $y$ and $z$ are held constant. Since $\\partial(e^{xz})/\\partial x=z e^{xz}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y e^{xz}+g(y,z).\n$$\n\nThis antiderivative is valid also at $z=0$; no division by $z$ is needed. Differentiating with respect to $y$ and matching the second component gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{xz}+g_y(y,z)&=e^{xz}+z,\\\\\ng_y(y,z)&=z,\\\\\ng(y,z)&=yz+h(z).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow $f=y e^{xz}+yz+h(z)$. When differentiating with respect to $z$, the exponential contributes a factor of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_z&=xy e^{xz}+y+h'(z),\\\\\nxy e^{xz}+y+h'(z)&=xy e^{xz}+y-2z,\\\\\nh'(z)&=-2z,\\\\\nh(z)&=-z^2+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y e^{xz}+yz-z^2+C.\n$$\n\nThe check is $f_x=yz e^{xz}$, $f_y=e^{xz}+z$, and $f_z=xy e^{xz}+y-2z$. All three components agree; the first component alone cannot determine the terms independent of $x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712573 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(1 - t\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=2 t + 1,\\qquad y=1 - t,\\qquad z=2 t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- z\\,i+- 2 y\\,j+2 x\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(1 - t\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- 2 t\\,i+\\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\,j+\\left(4 t + 2\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(1 - t\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =2\\,i+-\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(4 t + 2\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 t\\right)+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(- 2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(4\\right)+\\left(8 t\\right) \\\\ & =2 t + 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t + 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[t^{2} + 6 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{2} + 6 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} + 6 \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(7\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712575 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,j+\\left(y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- t - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(t - 1\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=- t - 1,\\qquad y=t - 1,\\qquad z=- 2 t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- 2 z\\,i+- 2 x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\left(- 2 t\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(- t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =4 t\\,i+\\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\,j+\\left(t - 1\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- t - 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t - 1\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =-\\,i+\\,j+-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(4 t\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(t - 1\\right)\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 t\\right)+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(- 2 t\\right) \\\\ & =4 - 4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(4 - 4 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- 2 t^{2} + 4 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 4 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- 2 \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 4 \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712582 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(2 z\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,j+\\left(y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(- t\\right)\\,j+\\left(1 - t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t - 1,\\qquad y=- t,\\qquad z=1 - t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2 z\\,i+2 x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(2 \\left(1 - t\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 \\left(t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(- t\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(2 - 2 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\,j+- t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t - 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- t\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(1 - t\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\,i+-\\,j+-\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(2 - 2 t\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- t\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2\\right)+\\left(- 2 t\\right)+\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 t\\right)+\\left(t\\right) \\\\ & =4 - 3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(4 - 3 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} + 4 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 4 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- \\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} + 4 \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712583 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- x\\right)\\,i+\\left(z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=2 t + 1,\\qquad y=2 t,\\qquad z=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- x\\,i+z\\,j+- 2 y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 t - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,j+- 4 t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- 2 t - 1\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 4 t\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-2\\right)+\\left(- 4 t\\right)+\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(4 t\\right)+\\left(- 8 t\\right) \\\\ & =- 8 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 8 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- 4 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- 4 \\left(0\\right)^{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712592 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(2 z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- 2 t - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(- t\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=- 2 t - 1,\\qquad y=- t,\\qquad z=2 t + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2 z\\,i+- 2 x\\,j+2 y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(- 2 t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 \\left(- t\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(4 t + 2\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t + 2\\right)\\,j+- 2 t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- 2 t - 1\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(- t\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =-2\\,i+-\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(4 t + 2\\right)\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(4 t + 2\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(- 8 t\\right)+\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 4 t\\right)+\\left(- 4 t\\right) \\\\ & =- 16 t - 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 16 t - 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- 8 t^{2} - 6 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 6 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- 8 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - 6 \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(-14\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712601 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(2 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- x\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\qquad y=t^{2},\\qquad z=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2 y\\,i+- 2 z\\,j+- x\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(2 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(t^{3}\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =2 t^{2}\\,i+- 2 t^{3}\\,j+- t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\,i+2 t\\,j+3 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 t^{3}\\right)\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- t\\right)\\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(- 4 t^{4}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 3 t^{3}\\right) \\\\ & =t^{2} \\left(- 4 t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(t^{2} \\left(- 4 t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 t^{4}}{4} + \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{4}}{4} + \\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{5}}{5} - \\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{4}}{4} + \\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{53}{60}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{53}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712603 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\qquad y=t^{2},\\qquad z=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+z\\,j+- y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(t\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =t\\,i+t^{3}\\,j+- t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\,i+2 t\\,j+3 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(t\\right)\\left(1\\right)+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- t^{2}\\right)\\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(t\\right)+\\left(2 t^{4}\\right)+\\left(- 3 t^{4}\\right) \\\\ & =- t^{4} + t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- t^{4} + t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{t^{5}}{5} + \\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{5}}{5} + \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- \\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{5}}{5} + \\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712607 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(z\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\qquad y=t^{2},\\qquad z=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+z\\,j+2 y\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(t\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =t\\,i+t^{3}\\,j+2 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\,i+2 t\\,j+3 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(t\\right)\\left(1\\right)+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(t\\right)+\\left(2 t^{4}\\right)+\\left(6 t^{4}\\right) \\\\ & =8 t^{4} + t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(8 t^{4} + t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{8 t^{5}}{5} + \\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\left(1\\right)^{5}}{5} + \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(\\frac{8 \\left(0\\right)^{5}}{5} + \\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{21}{10}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\frac{21}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712624 :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\qquad y=t^{2},\\qquad z=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =- y\\,i+- 2 x\\,j+- z\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- \\left(t^{3}\\right)\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =- t^{2}\\,i+- 2 t\\,j+- t^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,i \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\,i+2 t\\,j+3 t^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(- t^{2}\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- t^{3}\\right)\\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- t^{2}\\right)+\\left(- 4 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 3 t^{5}\\right) \\\\ & =t^{2} \\left(- 3 t^{3} - 5\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(t^{2} \\left(- 3 t^{3} - 5\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ & =\\left[- \\frac{t^{6}}{2} - \\frac{5 t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{6}}{2} - \\frac{5 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-\\left(- \\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{6}}{2} - \\frac{5 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- \\frac{13}{6}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712650 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2},\\,- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r_{x}'(t) & =4 t \\\\ r_{y}'(t) & =2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ r_{z}'(t) & =2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each derivative component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{x} & =16 t^{2} \\\\ S_{y} & =4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ S_{z} & =4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombine paired squares using $\\sin^2(t)+\\cos^2(t)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{S_x+S_y+S_z} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRetain the parameter bounds and integrate the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{-1}^{1}2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712657 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=(- t^{3})\\,i+(t)\\,j+(- t^{4})\\,k\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r_{x}'(t) & =- 3 t^{2} \\\\ r_{y}'(t) & =1 \\\\ r_{z}'(t) & =- 4 t^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each derivative component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{x} & =9 t^{4} \\\\ S_{y} & =1 \\\\ S_{z} & =16 t^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{S_x+S_y+S_z} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16 t^{6} + 9 t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRetain the parameter bounds and integrate the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{16 t^{6} + 9 t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712664 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n![The curve segment, parameter endpoints, and direction of travel.](images/9a/9af74cb2e6eb90342024f67f39b5e46b746d350ccb249af4390d45fbc058a91c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r_{x}'(t) & =4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ r_{y}'(t) & =2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each derivative component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{x} & =16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ S_{y} & =4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{S_x+S_y} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{\\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRetain the parameter bounds and integrate the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}2 \\sqrt{\\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712675 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=(- 2 e^{t})\\,i+(3 t e^{t})\\,j+(- 3 e^{t})\\,k\n$$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=1$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r_{x}'(t) & =- 2 e^{t} \\\\ r_{y}'(t) & =3 \\left(t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ r_{z}'(t) & =- 3 e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each derivative component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{x} & =4 e^{2 t} \\\\ S_{y} & =9 t^{2} e^{2 t} + 18 t e^{2 t} + 9 e^{2 t} \\\\ S_{z} & =9 e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{S_x+S_y+S_z} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9 t^{2} + 18 t + 22} e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\sqrt{e^{2t}}=e^t$ because $e^t>0$; the product square contributes $9 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2}$ inside the remaining radical.\n\nRetain the parameter bounds and integrate the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{9 t^{2} + 18 t + 22} e^{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712682 :question/problem "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\,j+(- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\,k\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\pi$? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n![The curve segment, parameter endpoints, and direction of travel.](images/24/24e8da3e728610a5ec6cdcbfe2c4b91aaba8ffe8b1cd218f7566a6cc238d2add.png)" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r_{x}'(t) & =2 \\\\ r_{y}'(t) & =4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ r_{z}'(t) & =- 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each derivative component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{x} & =4 \\\\ S_{y} & =16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ S_{z} & =16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombine paired squares using $\\sin^2(2 t)+\\cos^2(2 t)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{S_x+S_y+S_z} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRetain the parameter bounds and integrate the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sqrt{20}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712692 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f={}&\\bigl(\\cos(x+y)+2xy+y^2+2x\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i\\\\\n&+\\bigl(\\cos(x+y)+x^2+2xy+\\sin y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The components of $\\nabla f$ are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrating $f_x$ with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant, leaves an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\nIntegrating the first component term by term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int\\bigl(\\cos(x+y)+2xy+y^2+2x\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=\\sin(x+y)+x^2y+xy^2+x^2+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, $\\partial\\sin(x+y)/\\partial x=\\cos(x+y)$ because $\\partial(x+y)/\\partial x=1$.\n\nNow differentiate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\nf_y=\\cos(x+y)+x^2+2xy+g'(y).\n$$\n\nMatch this with the second component. All three terms depending on $x$ cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng'(y)&=\\cos(x+y)+x^2+2xy+\\sin y\\\\\n&\\quad-\\bigl(\\cos(x+y)+x^2+2xy\\bigr)\\\\\ng'(y)&=\\sin y\\\\\ng(y)&=-\\cos y+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sin(x+y)+x^2y+xy^2+x^2-\\cos y+C.\n$$\n\nChecking both partial derivatives gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=\\cos(x+y)+2xy+y^2+2x,\\\\\nf_y&=\\cos(x+y)+x^2+2xy+\\sin y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChanging $-\\cos y$ to $+\\cos y$ passes the first-component check but reverses the sign of $\\sin y$ in the second. Changing $+x^2$ to $-x^2$ passes the second-component check but reverses the sign of $2x$ in the first."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712694 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=(2xy)\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2-2y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives $f(x,y)=x^2y+g(y)$. Use the second component to determine $g(y)$ and select $f(x,y)$. In the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The components of $\\nabla f$ are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrating $f_x$ with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant, leaves an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\nHere $f_x=2xy$ and $f_y=x^2-2y$, so\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\int 2xy\\,\\mathrm dx=x^2y+g(y).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate with respect to $y$ and match the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+g'(y)&=x^2-2y\\\\\ng'(y)&=-2y\\\\\ng(y)&=-y^2+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y-y^2+C.\n$$\n\nChecking both components gives $f_x=2xy$ and $f_y=x^2-2y$, as required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712696 :question/problem "Find the general form of $f(x,y)$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f=(2xy+y^2+1)\\,\\mathbf i+(x^2+2xy+3y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIn the choices, $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "The components of $\\nabla f$ are $f_x$ and $f_y$. Integrating $f_x$ with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as constant, leaves an arbitrary function $g(y)$ because its $x$-derivative is zero.\n\nThe two required partial derivatives are $f_x=2xy+y^2+1$ and $f_y=x^2+2xy+3y^2$. Integrate the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\int(2xy+y^2+1)\\,\\mathrm dx\\\\\n&=x^2y+xy^2+x+g(y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression with respect to $y$ and using the second component gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+2xy+g'(y)&=x^2+2xy+3y^2\\\\\ng'(y)&=3y^2\\\\\ng(y)&=y^3+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute to obtain\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y+xy^2+x+y^3+C.\n$$\n\nVerification gives\n\n$$\nf_x=2xy+y^2+1,\\qquad f_y=x^2+2xy+3y^2.\n$$\n\nOmitting $y^3$ would preserve $f_x$ but fail to supply $3y^2$ in $f_y$. Omitting $x$ would preserve $f_y$ but lose the constant $1$ in $f_x$. Both components matter."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712709 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\\\ y(t)&=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z(t)&=- t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Use $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ z'(t)&=-1 \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2 \\\\ &=2 \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{-3}^{0}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{-3}^{0}(\\sqrt{2})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{2} t\\right]_{-3}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3\\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ &=3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712716 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=2 \\\\ y(t)&=- t^{2} \\\\ z(t)&=t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=4$ to $t=5$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=0 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 2 t \\\\ z'(t)&=2 t \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(0\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- 2 t\\right)^2+\\left(2 t\\right)^2 \\\\ &=8 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{8 t^{2}} \\\\ &=2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\ &=2\\sqrt{2} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{4}^{5}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{4}^{5}(2\\sqrt{2} t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{2} t^{2}\\right]_{4}^{5} \\\\ &=\\left(25\\sqrt{2}\\right)-\\left(16\\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ &=9\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712730 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=t^{2} - 4 \\\\ y(t)&=\\frac{t^{2}}{2} - 2 \\\\ z(t)&=- t^{2} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=-3$ to $t=-2$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\le 0$ on this interval, $|t|=-t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=2 t \\\\ y'(t)&=t \\\\ z'(t)&=- 2 t \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(2 t\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(t\\right)^2+\\left(- 2 t\\right)^2 \\\\ &=9 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{9 t^{2}} \\\\ &=3|t| \\\\ &=- 3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{-3}^{-2}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{-3}^{-2}(- 3 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{-3}^{-2} \\\\ &=\\left(-6\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{27}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712738 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=- 2 t \\\\ y(t)&=t^{2} \\\\ z(t)&=2 - \\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=4$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. The quadratic inside the square is positive for every real $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=-2 \\\\ y'(t)&=2 t \\\\ z'(t)&=- t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(-2\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(2 t\\right)^2+\\left(- t^{2}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=t^{4} + 4 t^{2} + 4 \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{t^{4} + 4 t^{2} + 4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(t^{2} + 2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=t^{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{2}^{4}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(t^{2} + 2)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{t \\left(t^{2} + 6\\right)}{3}\\right]_{2}^{4} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{88}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{20}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{68}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712740 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=\\sqrt{6} t^{2} - 4\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\ y(t)&=- 2 t - 8 \\\\ z(t)&=2 - 2 t^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=3$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. The quadratic inside the square is positive for every real $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=2\\sqrt{6} t \\\\ y'(t)&=-2 \\\\ z'(t)&=- 6 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(2\\sqrt{6} t\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(- 6 t^{2}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=36 t^{4} + 24 t^{2} + 4 \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{36 t^{4} + 24 t^{2} + 4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(6 t^{2} + 2\\right)^2} \\\\ &=6 t^{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{2}^{3}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{3}(6 t^{2} + 2)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 t \\left(t^{2} + 1\\right)\\right]_{2}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left(60\\right)-\\left(20\\right) \\\\ &=40\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712747 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y(t)&=3 t^{2} + 2 \\\\ z(t)&=4 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=3$ to $t=5$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=2\\left(2\\sin(2t)+4t\\cos(2t)-2\\sin(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=8 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=6 t \\\\ z'(t)&=2\\left(2\\cos(2t)-4t\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(8 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(6 t\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=100 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{100 t^{2}} \\\\ &=10|t| \\\\ &=10 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{3}^{5}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{3}^{5}(10 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[5 t^{2}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\left(125\\right)-\\left(45\\right) \\\\ &=80\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712757 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=- 4 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\\\ y(t)&=- 4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 2 \\\\ z(t)&=3 t^{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=3$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=-2\\left(2\\cos(2t)-4t\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=8 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=-2\\left(2\\sin(2t)+4t\\cos(2t)-2\\sin(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ z'(t)&=6 t \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(8 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- 8 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2+\\left(6 t\\right)^2 \\\\ &=100 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{100 t^{2}} \\\\ &=10|t| \\\\ &=10 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{0}^{3}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(10 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[5 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left(45\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=45\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186712762 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve with coordinate functions\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t)&=2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y(t)&=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - 2 \\\\ z(t)&=- 2 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=4$ to $t=5$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the coordinates and simplify the speed. Since $t\\ge 0$ on this interval, $|t|=t$. Use $\\sin^2(\\theta)+\\cos^2(\\theta)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=\\left(2\\cos(2t)-4t\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ y'(t)&=3 t \\\\ z'(t)&=-1\\left(2\\sin(2t)+4t\\cos(2t)-2\\sin(2t)\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^2&=\\left(- 4 t \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(3 t\\right)^2+\\left(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)^2 \\\\ &=25 t^{2} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{25 t^{2}} \\\\ &=5|t| \\\\ &=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L&=\\int_{4}^{5}\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{4}^{5}(5 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{4}^{5} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{125}{2}\\right)-\\left(40\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{45}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
